    "doomIndexInputSummary": "FCC signs off on SpaceX’s plan to put up to 1 million data center satellites into space. In an announcement on Wednesday, the FCC says it authorized SpaceX to deploy up to 1 million AI chip-equipped satellites as part of the Elon Musk-owned company's \"Starmind\" initiative. With several others also pursuing the idea of putting AI data centers in space, including Google and Blue Origin, this is the first approval the agency has granted for a full \"constellation\" of AI satellites. The FCC's approval requires SpaceX to deploy at least one satellite by 2033, and meet the 1 million satellite milestone by 2040. The FCC also deferred SpaceX's request to dispose of satellites located above 600km in space \"graveyard\" orbits, rather than … Read the full story at The Verge. In an announcement on Wednesday, the FCC says it authorized SpaceX to deploy up to 1 million AI chip-equipped satellites as part of the Elon Musk-owned company's \"Starmind\" initiative. With several others also pursuing the idea of putting AI data centers in space, including Google and Blue Origin, this is the first approval the agency […] Musk's plan to send a million SpaceX data satellites into space approved. The Starmind constellation aims to be the most cost and energy-efficient way to meet the growing demand for AI computing power. FCC clears SpaceX to put up to 1 million AI satellites in orbit. The FCC approved SpaceX’s plan for up to 1 million Starmind satellites. Here’s how its space-based AI data centers would work and why astronomers object."
